            <div class="zn-element zn-lg  ">
              <div id="" class="zn-text "   ><p>The last days of winter can be tough. Even as we scan the ground for crocuses, obsessive in anticipation of spring, any sign of spring, we’re often still wearing hefty sweaters and gloves. </p><p><br></p><p>After checking every forsythia limb in my yard for buds and peering into every treetop for swallows, I often go back inside shivering. I light my fireplace and look for something warm to drink. Well, not just warm. Boiled lettuce water is warm. I mean cozy, a beverage that soothes not only my body but also my soul, one that gets me through winter’s final throes.</p><p><br></p><p>As luck would have it, we recently discovered a new craftsperson who specializes in cozy. Katie-lynn McDonald, who founded <a href="https://woodshed.life/collections/vendors?q=Full%20Moon%20Tea%20Company">Full Moon Tea Company</a> in Asheville, North Carolina, is a former pastry chef who’s always been passionate about what she calls “flirty flavors,” unlikely combos that tantalize the senses. </p><p><br></p><p>Katie nailed it with this recipe, which marries the smoky warmth of her signature <a href="/products/smoky-mountains-cowboy-chai-tea">Smoky Mountains Cowboy Chai Tea</a> with unlikely ingredients like orange juice, a piquant yet sweet counterpoint that brightens each sip and, for me, foreshadows the coming spring. Katie’s unlikely flavor combo gives these seemingly endless late-winter days a bit of zip, and with mug after mug of it in my hands, I just might make it through to April.&nbsp;</p></div>
            </div>
            
            
